2026-10-16T15:08:21.0800922Z   ISSUE_NUMBER: 56115
2026-10-16T15:08:21.0801107Z   ISSUE_TITLE: Plugin host API: expose inbox cancel and a busy query to plugins
2026-10-16T15:08:21.0804375Z   ISSUE_BODY: ### Plugin host API: expose inbox cancel and a busy query to plugins

**Context.** Plugins that construct parent notifications for background children currently burn agent turns on stale signals. Concrete case: alvinunreal/oh-my-opencode-slim#1504 — a child permission ask resolved by an external auto-responder still produces a queued parent wake that cannot be retracted, so the parent wakes and spends a turn on an already-resolved ask. We fixed the plugin-side policy, but the remaining fix (retracting a queued wake by ID, and not waking a busy parent) needs two primitives that **already exist in v2 core** but are not exposed on the plugin host `ctx`:

1. **`session.inbox.cancel(inboxID)`** — `SessionInbox.cancel` exists (`packages/core/src/session/inbox.ts:230`, pre-delivery only; already-delivered → `LifecycleConflict`) and is served over HTTP (`DELETE /api/session/:sessionID/inbox/:inboxID`), but the plugin host session domain (`packages/core/src/plugin/host.ts:504-525`) exposes no inbox operations. A plugin that records the inbox/message ID returned by its own `prompt`/`synthetic` admission could then retract a queued notice the moment the underlying request resolves.

2. **An authoritative busy query** — `execution.isActive` and the `GET /api/session/active` listing exist, but plugin ctx offers no authoritative query — only event-tracked heuristics or a racing `wait`. One authoritative check before admitting a wake would prevent waking a busy parent.

**Use case.** Stop spending orchestrator turns on child permission asks that were resolved externally after a fixed settle window; retract queued stale wakes by ID instead of layering settle timers and heuristics in plugin land. (Per-call `resume:false` admission on `ctx.session.prompt`/`synthetic` already helps here and is in active use.)

**Boundary note.** Mechanism belongs in the host (it owns inbox state and execution busy); policy stays in the plugin. Both are thin exposures of existing internals — no new core behavior needed. Checked against v2 pinned commit `dbd9b18f3d7b4ef3e346eafd91613896c9e3c864`.

